<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<RunSettings>
  <DataCollectionRunSettings>
    <DataCollectors>
      <DataCollector friendlyName="XPlat code coverage">
        <Configuration>
          <Format>cobertura</Format>
          <!-- What the 80% gate is measured over. Everything named here is deliberately
               outside the figure; anything not named is in it, tested or not.

               Aspire orchestration: a dozen lines that only mean anything once the real
               containers are up, so there is nothing here a unit test could reach.

               The host and ServiceDefaults are composition and unmodified Aspire
               template code — the same argument. They are named rather than left out
               by accident: neither is referenced by the test project today, so coverlet
               never loads them, and a single ProjectReference added later would drop
               180 uncovered lines into the figure without anything being less tested. -->
          <Exclude>[SevenBySeven.AppHost]*,[SevenBySeven.Web]*,[SevenBySeven.ServiceDefaults]*</Exclude>
          <!-- EF writes the migrations and the model snapshot, and they outnumber the
               code by some margin. Counting them buries the figure that matters: a new
               migration would drop the percentage without anything being less tested.

               Razor components are markup with a tail of code. Coverlet counts the
               generated render tree, so their percentage measures how much HTML a test
               rendered rather than whether anything behaves correctly — see
               docs/adr/0004. Logic worth testing is lifted out into the module instead,
               as Scan.FromJpeg already is. -->
          <ExcludeByFile>**/Migrations/*.cs,**/*.razor</ExcludeByFile>
        </Configuration>
      </DataCollector>
    </DataCollectors>
  </DataCollectionRunSettings>
</RunSettings>
